onstrate error detection. Aim: To implement CRC in Java and demonstrate how it detects errors during data transmission. CRC uses modulo-2 binary division with XOR. A zero remainder means no error was detected; a nonzero remainder indicates an error. Step 1: Create CRC.java public class CRC {    static String divide(String data, String generator) {        char[] bits = data.toCharArray();        int n = generator.length();        for (int i = 0; i <= bits.length - n; i++) {            if (bits[i] == '1') {                for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {                    bits[i + j] = (bits[i + j] == generator.charAt(j))                            ? '0' : '1';                }            }        }        return new String(bits, bits.length - n + 1, n - 1);    }    static boolean hasError(String received, String generator) {        return !divide(received, generator).matches("0+");    }    public static void main(String[] args) {        String data = "1101011011";        String generator = "10011";        System.out.println("Original Data: " + data);        System.out.println("Generator:     " + generator);        String appendedData = data + "0".repeat(generator.length() - 1);        String crc = divide(appendedData, generator);        String transmitted = data + crc;        System.out.println("\\nCRC Remainder: " + crc);        System.out.println("Transmitted Data: " + transmitted);        System.out.println("\\n--- Test 1: Without Error ---");        System.out.println("Received Data: " + transmitted);        System.out.println(hasError(transmitted, generator)                ? "Error Detected!" : "No Error Detected.");        System.out.println("\\n--- Test 2: With Error ---");        char[] corrupted = transmitted.toCharArray();        corrupted[0] = corrupted[0] == '0' ? '1' : '0';        String receivedWithError = new String(corrupted);        System.out.println("Received Data: " + receivedWithError);        System.out.println(hasError(receivedWithError, generator)                ? "Error Detected!" : "No Error Detected.");    } } Page 2 Java Networking & IPC PracticalsStep 2: Compile and run javac CRC.java java CRC Expected output Original Data: 1101011011 Generator:     10011 CRC Remainder: 1110 Transmitted Data: 11010110111110--- Test 1: Without Error --Received Data: 11010110111110 No Error Detected.--- Test 2: With Error --Received Data: 01010110111110 Error Detected! Viva: CRC means Cyclic Redundancy Check; XOR is used in modulo-2 division; CRC detects but does not itself correct errors. Result: CRC bits are calculated and a flipped bit is detected. 3. Implement the Stop-and-Wait ARQ protocol using Java and demonstrate frame transmission and acknowledgement. Aim: To simulate Stop-and-Wait ARQ, including frame transmission, acknowledgements, lost ACK, timeout, and retransmission. The sender sends one frame and waits for its ACK before sending the next frame. Step 1: Create StopWaitARQ.java public class StopWaitARQ {    public static void main(String[] args) {        int totalFrames = 4;        int expectedSeq = 0;        boolean ackLostOnce = false;        System.out.println("Stop-and-Wait ARQ Protocol");        System.out.println("--------------------------");        for (int frame = 1; frame <= totalFrames; frame++) {            int seq = (frame - 1) % 2;            boolean acknowledged = false;            while (!acknowledged) {                System.out.println("\\nSender: Sending Frame "                        + frame + " (Seq=" + seq + ")");                if (seq == expectedSeq) {                    System.out.println("Receiver: Frame "                            + frame + " received successfully.");                    expectedSeq = 1 - expectedSeq;                } else {                    System.out.println("Receiver: Duplicate frame detected.");                }                if (frame == 2 && !ackLostOnce) {                    System.out.println("ACK " + seq + " lost!");                    System.out.println("Sender: Timeout. Retransmitting frame "                            + frame + ".");                    ackLostOnce = true;                    continue;                }                System.out.println("Receiver: Sending ACK " + seq);                System.out.println("Sender: ACK " + seq + " received.");                acknowledged = true;            }        }        System.out.println("\\nAll frames transmitted successfully.");    } } Step 2: Compile and run javac StopWaitARQ.java java StopWaitARQ Expected behavior Frame 1 is sent and acknowledged. The first ACK for Frame 2 is deliberately lost, so the sender retransmits Frame 2. The receiver recognizes the duplicate and acknowledges it again. Frames 3 and 4 then complete. Page 3 Java Networking & IPC PracticalsViva: ACK confirms reception; lost ACK causes timeout and retransmission; sequence numbers help identify duplicates. Result: Stop-and-Wait ARQ behavior is demonstrated by simulation. 4. Develop a TCP Daytime Server program using C/Java that sends the current date and time to a client. Aim: To create a TCP server that sends the current date and time to a client. The Java server listens on port 5000 and sends a formatted date-time string to each connecting client. Step 1: Create files DaytimeServer.java DaytimeClient.java Step 2: DaytimeServer.java import java.io.PrintWriter; import java.net.ServerSocket; import java.net.Socket; import java.time.LocalDateTime; import java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter; public class DaytimeServer {    public static void main(String[] args) {        int port = 5000;        try (ServerSocket serverSocket = new ServerSocket(port)) {            System.out.println("Daytime Server started.");            System.out.println("Waiting for client...");            while (true) {                try (Socket socket = serverSocket.accept();                     PrintWriter output = new PrintWriter(                             socket.getOutputStream(), true)) {                    System.out.println("Client connected.");                    String dateTime = LocalDateTime.now().format(                            DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("dd-MM-yyyy HH:mm:ss"));                    output.println("Current Date and Time: " + dateTime);                    System.out.println("Date and time sent: " + dateTime);                }            }        } catch (Exception e) {            System.out.println("Server error: " + e.getMessage());        }    } } Step 3: DaytimeClient.java import java.io.BufferedReader; import java.io.InputStreamReader; import java.net.Socket; public class DaytimeClient {    public static void main(String[] args) {        String host = "localhost";        int port = 5000;        try (Socket socket = new Socket(host, port);             BufferedReader input = new BufferedReader(                     new InputStreamReader(socket.getInputStream()))) {            System.out.println("Connected to Daytime Server.");            System.out.println(input.readLine());        } catch (Exception e) {            System.out.println("Client error: " + e.getMessage());            System.out.println("Start the server before the client.");        }    } } Step 4: Compile and run javac DaytimeServer.java DaytimeClient.java # Terminal 1 java DaytimeServer # Terminal 2 java DaytimeClient Expected output Connected to Daytime Server. Current Date and Time: 28-10-2026 09:00:00 (The date/time will match your computer clock.) Troubleshooting: If 'Address already in use' appears, stop the old server with Ctrl+C or change the port in both files. Result: The server sends the current date and time over TCP. Page 4 Java Networking & IPC Practicals5. Implement the Sliding Window protocol using Java and demonstrate transmission of multiple frames and acknowledgements. Aim: To simulate the Sliding Window protocol by transmitting multiple frames and displaying acknowledgements. The sender can send several frames within a window before moving the window forward after ACKs. Step 1: Create SlidingWindow.java public class SlidingWindow {    public static void main(String[] args) {        int totalFrames = 8;        int windowSize = 3;        int base = 1;        System.out.println("Sliding Window Protocol");        System.out.println("-----------------------");        System.out.println("Total Frames: " + totalFrames);        System.out.println("Window Size: " + windowSize);        while (base <= totalFrames) {            int end = Math.min(base + windowSize - 1, totalFrames);            System.out.println("\\nSender: Sending frames " + base + " to " + end);            for (int frame = base; frame <= end; frame++)                System.out.println("Sender -> Receiver: Frame " + frame);            for (int frame = base; frame <= end; frame++)                System.out.println("Receiver -> Sender: ACK " + frame);            base = end + 1;            System.out.println("Window slides forward.");        }        System.out.println("\\nAll frames transmitted successfully.");    } } Step 2: Compile and run javac SlidingWindow.java java SlidingWindow Expected output Sliding Window Protocol----------------------Total Frames: 8 Window Size: 3 Sender: Sending frames 1 to 3 Sender -> Receiver: Frame 1 Sender -> Receiver: Frame 2 Sender -> Receiver: Frame 3 Receiver -> Sender: ACK 1 Receiver -> Sender: ACK 2 Receiver -> Sender: ACK 3 Window slides forward. Sender: Sending frames 4 to 6 Sender -> Receiver: Frame 4 Sender -> Receiver: Frame 5 Sender -> Receiver: Frame 6 Receiver -> Sender: ACK 4 Receiver -> Sender: ACK 5 Receiver -> Sender: ACK 6 Window slides forward. Sender: Sending frames 7 to 8 Sender -> Receiver: Frame 7 Sender -> Receiver: Frame 8 Receiver -> Sender: ACK 7 Receiver -> Sender: ACK 8 Window slides forward. All frames transmitted successfully. Viva: Window size is the maximum number of outstanding frames; ACK confirms reception; sliding windows improve efficiency compared with Stop-and-Wait. Result: Multiple frame transmission, acknowledgements, and window movement are simulated. Page 5 Java Networking & IPC Practicals6. Develop a TCP Echo Server program using C/Java that receives a message from a client and returns it. Aim: To create a TCP Echo Server that sends each received client message back unchanged. The server listens on port 5001 and echoes messages. The word exit ends the connection. Step 1: Create files EchoServer.java EchoClient.java Step 2: EchoServer.java import java.io.*; import java.net.*; public class EchoServer {    public static void main(String[] args) {        int port = 5001;        try (ServerSocket serverSocket = new ServerSocket(port)) {            System.out.println("TCP Echo Server started.");            System.out.println("Waiting for client...");            try (Socket socket = serverSocket.accept();                 BufferedReader input = new BufferedReader(                     new InputStreamReader(socket.getInputStream()));                 PrintWriter output = new PrintWriter(                     socket.getOutputStream(), true)) {                System.out.println("Client connected.");                String message;                while ((message = input.readLine()) != null) {                    System.out.println("Client: " + message);                    if (message.equalsIgnoreCase("exit")) {                        output.println("Goodbye!");                        break;                    }                    output.println(message);                }                System.out.println("Client disconnected.");            }        } catch (IOException e) {            System.out.println("Server error: " + e.getMessage());        }    } } Step 3: EchoClient.java import java.io.*; import java.net.*; import java.util.Scanner; public class EchoClient {    public static void main(String[] args) {        String host = "localhost";        int port = 5001;        try (Socket socket = new Socket(host, port);             BufferedReader input = new BufferedReader(                 new InputStreamReader(socket.getInputStream()));             PrintWriter output = new PrintWriter(                 socket.getOutputStream(), true);             Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in)) {            System.out.println("Connected to Echo Server.");            while (true) {                System.out.print("Enter message (exit to stop): ");                String message = scanner.nextLine();                output.println(message);                System.out.println("Echo from server: " + input.readLine());                if (message.equalsIgnoreCase("exit")) break;            }        } catch (IOException e) {            System.out.println("Client error: " + e.getMessage());            System.out.println("Start the server first.");        }    } } Step 4: Compile and run javac EchoServer.java EchoClient.java # Terminal 1 java EchoServer # Terminal 2 java EchoClient Expected output Connected to Echo Server. Enter message (exit to stop): Hello Server Echo from server: Hello Server Page 6 Java Networking & IPC PracticalsEnter message (exit to stop): How are you? Echo from server: How are you? Enter message (exit to stop): exit Echo from server: Goodbye! Result: The server receives messages and returns the same text to the client. 7. Implement an IPC Message Queue program using Java to exchange messages between processes. Aim: To simulate an Inter-Process Communication message queue using Java and exchange messages between two separate processes. This practical uses a shared file with file locks as a simple file-backed queue. It is not a native OS message queue. Step 1: Create project IPCMessageQueue/    MessageQueue.java    messages.txt (created automatically) Step 2: MessageQueue.java import java.io.*; import java.nio.ByteBuffer; import java.nio.channels.FileChannel; import java.nio.channels.FileLock; import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets; import java.nio.file.*; import java.util.Scanner; public class MessageQueue {    private static final Path QUEUE = Paths.get("messages.txt");    static void sendMessage(String message) throws IOException {        byte[] data = (message + "\n").getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8);        try (FileChannel channel = FileChannel.open(QUEUE,                StandardOpenOption.CREATE, StandardOpenOption.READ,                StandardOpenOption.WRITE);             FileLock lock = channel.lock()) {            channel.position(channel.size());            channel.write(ByteBuffer.wrap(data));        }    }    static String receiveMessages() throws IOException {        try (FileChannel channel = FileChannel.open(QUEUE,                StandardOpenOption.CREATE, StandardOpenOption.READ,                StandardOpenOption.WRITE);             FileLock lock = channel.lock()) {            long size = channel.size();            if (size == 0) return "";            ByteBuffer buffer = ByteBuffer.allocate((int) size);            channel.position(0);            while (buffer.hasRemaining() && channel.read(buffer) != -1) { }            String messages = new String(buffer.array(), 0, buffer.position(),                    StandardCharsets.UTF_8);            channel.truncate(0);            return messages;        }    }    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {        if (args.length != 1 ||                !(args[0].equalsIgnoreCase("send") ||                  args[0].equalsIgnoreCase("receive"))) {            System.out.println("Usage: java MessageQueue send");            System.out.println("   or: java MessageQueue receive");            return;        }        if (args[0].equalsIgnoreCase("send")) {            Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);            System.out.println("Sender process started.");            System.out.println("Type a message; type 'exit' to stop.");            while (true) {                System.out.print("Enter message: ");                String message = scanner.nextLine();                if (message.equalsIgnoreCase("exit")) break;                sendMessage(message);                System.out.println("Message added to queue.");            }            System.out.println("Sender process stopped.");        } else {            System.out.println("Receiver process started.");            System.out.println("Waiting for messages...");            while (true) {                String messages = receiveMessages();                if (!messages.isEmpty()) {                    for (String message : messages.split("\\\\R")) {                        if (!message.isEmpty()) Page 7 Java Networking & IPC Practicals                    }                            System.out.println("Received: " + message);                }                Thread.sleep(500);            }        }    } } Step 3: Compile javac MessageQueue.java Step 4: Start receiver in Terminal 1 java MessageQueue receive Step 5: Start sender in Terminal 2 java MessageQueue send Step 6: Example output Receiver terminal: Receiver process started. Waiting for messages... Received: Hello Receiver Received: How are you? Sender terminal: Sender process started. Type a message; type 'exit' to stop. Enter message: Hello Receiver Message added to queue. Enter message: How are you? Message added to queue. Enter message: exit Sender process stopped. The receiver checks the queue every 500 ms. Stop it with Ctrl+C. File locking helps prevent simultaneous access to the shared file. Step 7: Viva questions What is IPC? Inter-Process Communication allows processes to exchange data. What is a message queue? A queue stores messages until another process retrieves them. Why use file locking? It helps prevent the sender and receiver from modifying the shared queue file simultaneously. Is this a native OS message queue? No. It is a file-backed simulation. Native queues require OS-specific APIs or a library. Result: Two separate Java processes exchange messages through a shared file-backed queue. General VS Code troubleshooting 'javac' is not recognized: Install a JDK and configure PATH, then restart VS Code. Connection refused: Start the server before the client. Address already in use: Stop the previous server with Ctrl+C or change the port in both client and server files. Could not find or load main class: Run commands from the project folder and compile the Java files first.
